# Memory Management: Fitting Programs into RAM

> RAM is small, shared, and contested. The memory manager tracks
> every byte, protects neighbours — and lets programs use more memory
> than the machine physically has.

## 1. The Job Description

The memory manager must **track**, **protect**, **relocate**, and
**share** memory. When addresses get bound matters:

| Binding time | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| Compile time | Compiler fixes absolute addresses; program must load at one spot |
| Load time | Addresses patched at load; program may load anywhere |
| Execution time | Hardware translates every address; process may move |

## 2. Logical vs Physical Addresses

A **logical (virtual) address** is what the program sees; the
**physical address** is the real RAM location. The **MMU** — the
memory management unit between CPU and RAM — translates between them
at run time; in its simplest form one **relocation register** is
added to every address emitted.

[definition]
The **address space** is the range of logical addresses a process may
use. Two processes can both place a variable at address 4000 — the
MMU maps each to a different physical home.
[/definition]

## 3. Contiguous Allocation and Fragmentation

The oldest scheme gives each process one contiguous block of RAM —
so which free hole should the allocator choose?

| Strategy | Picks | Weakness |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First fit | First hole big enough | Fast, but scatters small holes |
| Best fit | Smallest hole that fits | Leaves useless slivers |
| Worst fit | Largest hole | Remainders still may not fit |

| Type | What breaks | Cure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **External** | Free memory lies in scattered, small holes | Compaction — shuffle processes together |
| **Internal** | Allocated block exceeds the request; slack is wasted inside | Smaller allocation units |

**Compaction** works only because addresses bind at execution time —
and it burns CPU moving live processes.

## 4. Paging: Fixed-Size Blocks

**Paging** slices logical memory into equal **pages** and physical RAM
into same-size **frames**; any page may occupy any free frame, so
external fragmentation vanishes — only the last page of a process
wastes part of a frame (**internal fragmentation**). The per-process
**page table** maps page numbers to frame numbers; the MMU splits
each virtual address into (page number, offset), and every address
flows through the **TLB** — a small cache of recent translations:

@diagram tree id="address-translation" layout="tree" aria="Virtual address translated through TLB and page table to a physical address"
  "CPU emits virtual address" -> "TLB lookup"
  "TLB lookup" -> "TLB hit (fast)"
  "TLB lookup" -> "TLB miss: walk the page table"
  "TLB hit (fast)" -> "Physical frame + offset"
  "TLB miss: walk the page table" -> "Physical frame + offset"
@/diagram

[example]
With a TLB lookup of 20 ns, memory access of 100 ns, and an 80% hit
ratio: a hit costs 20 + 100 = 120 ns; a miss costs 20 + 100 + 100 =
220 ns. Effective access time = 0.8 × 120 + 0.2 × 220 = **140 ns**.
[/example]

Small pages waste less internally but bloat the page table; large
pages do the reverse — the classic **page size trade-off**.

## 5. Virtual Memory and Demand Paging

**Virtual memory** lets a program run while only some pages are in
RAM. A **page fault** occurs when the CPU touches a page whose
valid bit says "not in memory":

```text
1. Trap to the OS; save registers; verify the access is legal
2. Find the page on disk
3. Find a free frame — or evict a victim (write back if dirty)
4. Read the page from disk into the frame; update the page table
5. Set the valid bit; restart the faulting instruction
```

[important]
The instruction restart is the trick that makes demand paging work:
the process never knows the fault happened — but disk is roughly a
million times slower than RAM, so health requires rare faults.
[/important]

## 6. Page Replacement Algorithms

When no free frame exists, the OS must evict a victim — on the
reference string `1 2 3 4 1 2 5 1 2 3 4 5` with 3 frames:

| Algorithm | Rule | Faults | Comment |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| FIFO | Evict the oldest page loaded | 9 | Suffers Belady's anomaly |
| LRU | Evict least recently used | 10 | Needs age tracking |
| Optimal | Evict the page not needed for longest | 7 | Unimplementable — benchmark only |

[warning]
**Belady's anomaly**: with FIFO, more frames can mean more faults —
that same reference string faults 10 times with 4 frames but 9 with
3. LRU and Optimal are immune to the anomaly.
[/warning]

## 7. Thrashing and the Working Set

**Thrashing** is the spiral where a process holds too few frames: it
faults, evicts a page it needs moments later, faults again — the CPU
services faults instead of executing. The **working set** — pages
used in the last Δ references — is the memory truly needed.

## 8. Paging vs Segmentation

**Segmentation** divides memory by *meaning* — a code segment, a
stack, a heap — in variable sizes; x86 does both: segment logically,
then page each segment.

| Aspect | Paging | Segmentation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unit | Fixed-size page | Variable-size logical segment |
| Fragmentation | Internal only, in last page | External possible |

## Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| MMU | Translates logical to physical addresses at run time |
| External vs internal | Scattered holes vs wasted slack inside |
| Page fault | Load missing page from disk, restart instruction |
| FIFO / LRU / Optimal | 9 / 10 / 7 faults on the classic string |
